From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 12:08:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/imagination: apple: Report the A8's faults and hangs and
 recover from them

On the A8 a fault in a job's context can leave the firmware silent: a
write the MCU made through a job's context to an unmapped page left
EVENT_STATUS bit 9 (MMU page fault) latched for 1.5 s with no event and
no watchdog. A firmware fault event printed its raw bytes and then "GPU
MMU fault licence refused: no licence for this chip", as the A10's
licence check has no A8 counterpart, so the fault registers were never
read at the event. And a fault reset went through the wrong job:

- At a job timeout or a firmware fault, report each engine's submitted
  job, stamps and work ring from memory, then read EVENT_STATUS once,
  gated as every access to the A8's Rogue registers (Fender state 1,
  PLL locked), and only with bit 9 set the BIF's two fault banks once
  each: context, address, read or write, tag and sideband. Once per
  firmware start, never in a loop: a tight poll of these registers
  during a job froze the whole SoC, while single reads after an event
  and at a timeout are safe. The timeout report skips the registers
  only when the fault event's read happened.
- A chip may capture the fault itself (&pvr_apple_chip.report_fault)
  in place of the licence-checked bank read; only the T7000 sets it.
- A type-4 event (the firmware's lockup recovery) is decoded from
  memory: the arm (+8: 0 the DM handler's MMU-fault arm, which took
  bit 9 itself, so the banks are read on its word; 1 the TA/3D
  watchdog), the +0x24 word one byte per group (TA, 3D, CL) with the
  locked one named, object 0x4b raw, and each engine whose submitted and
  done counts differ.
- A hang reports the parameter manager's state from memory (the TA
  object's out-of-memory and watchdog words, the last geometry VM's PM
  control, usage words and stack top), then the PM's live top index once
  through the gate. A stall for pages doubles the pool of later VMs.
- A firmware fault reset through the oldest pending job of any engine,
  including one its engine had finished but the poll had not retired
  yet: on a texture test the geometry job was done and the fragment job
  hung, the fault went to the geometry scheduler, found no job there,
  and the fragment job waited for its 5 s timeout. A chip with
  &pvr_apple_chip.fault_skips_done (the T7000) takes each engine's first
  unfinished job instead.
- pvr_apple_wait_idle() waited up to its timeout per engine, so a
  closing client and the VM it frees each held submit_lock for up to
  3 x 5 s. A chip with &pvr_apple_chip.wait_deadline waits once, with
  one deadline for all engines, and a wait that gives up names the
  engine, its counts and the fault flag, from memory only. A submit
  refused before the chip's own path (firmware not running, VM unknown,
  every VM slot taken) says why, and the A8's geometry preparation takes
  its VM's lock killably, as the holder may wait on the GPU.

The A9 and A10 take the unchanged paths: the new hooks and flags are set
only in the T7000 table.

Assisted-by: LLM
